  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 200 PM Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Nearly the entire forecast area is now under either \na level 1 or 2 risk for severe thunderstorms Thursday afternoon.\n\nAhead of an approaching cold front, an area of showers and \nthunderstorms is expected to develop across the mid-Atlantic states, \nextending southwest into the Carolinas. With the afternoon update, \nthe Storm Prediction Center has increased the severe risk across \nmost of the area for Thursday afternoon and evening. While their \nprevious issuance included a marginal (level 1 of 5) risk from Wake \nCounty to the northeast, the newest issuance now has a slight (level \n2 of 5) risk from Franklin County to the northeast, with a marginal \nrisk elsewhere. While large hail cannot be ruled out northeast of \nRaleigh, the primary threat of severe weather will come from \ndamaging wind gusts. The newest runs of the HRW NSSL and HRW ARW \nshow that convection could start as early as 1-2pm along and west of \nthe US-1 corridor, although the primary threat for severe weather \nwill occur between 5-9pm. \n\n\nKey message 2... One more day of above normal temperatures is \nexpected, with forecast highs in the 90s everywhere on Thursday.\n\nA Heat Advisory remains in effect through 8pm tonight for Cumberland \nand Sampson counties - although heat index values may not reach 105 \nin these counties due to lower dewpoints and moisture, the \nexperimental HeatRisk product indicates an extreme (level 4 of 4) \nrisk for portions of these counties. While dewpoints may increase by \na degree or two on Thursday, air temperatures are expected to rise \nby a couple degrees on Thursday compared to today. A Heat Advisory \nwas issued for Cumberland and Sampson counties again, in addition to \nHarnett and Wayne counties. While it is possible that other areas \ncould have the heat index rise to 105 degrees, there is more \nuncertainty outside of these 4 counties due to the potential for \nconvection beginning earlier in the afternoon. If confidence \nincreases that thunderstorms will be delayed in arriving Thursday \nafternoon, later shifts may choose to expand the heat advisory.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... Rain/thunderstorm chances will increase Friday and \nSaturday. Flash flooding and strong to damaging winds will be the \nmain threats.  \n\nAn upper level trough will remain located over the Eastern US into \nnext week. There should be a few mid/upper level shortwave troughs \nembedded within the upper flow, bringing a source of lift for a few \nrounds of rain. The best chance for widespread rain will be on \nFriday. A very moist airmass will be in place, as seen by an \nincrease of PWs to 2 to 2.1 in over the region. Current guidance \nsuggests widespread rainfall totals of around 1-2 inches on Friday, \nwith locally higher amounts possible where stronger cells train over \nthe same area. Thus, mostly localized flash flooding will be \npossible, especially in urban and poor drainage areas. Additionally, \nthere should be adequate instability with the 50th percentile of the \nLREF suggesting around 2500 J/kg of CAPE. This combined with around \n20-30 kts of bulk shear should be sufficient for a few stronger \ncells to form and sustain over the region Friday afternoon and \nevening. Depending on the timing of any rain on Saturday, a similar \nairmass will be in place, meaning that additional strong to severe \nstorms will be possible. With any severe storms that occur this \nweekend, the main threat will be damaging winds, especially from \nwater-loaded downdrafts. Additionally, cloud cover could cause \nlowered temperatures and instability, which would lessen the threat \nof any severe weather both Friday and Saturday, so will need to see \ntrends in the guidance as we get closer. The trough axis should \nshift eastward Sunday into Monday, bringing drier air to the region \nand lessening rain chances at the start of the work week. Another \nshortwave trough may pass over the region, bringing the next best \nchance of rain. \n\nWith the trough overhead, temperatures should fall back closer to \nnormal for this time of year. This equates to high temperatures \ngenerally being in the mid 80s to low 90s through the weekend into \nnext week.",
